    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. Seven escalation handlers were still returning plain `String`/`Map` bodies:\n\n- **400** \u2014 `MissingOwnerFieldsHandler` (also fires for the disposable-email and postcode checks, which reuse `MissingOwnerFieldsException`)\n- **409** \u2014 `CityAtCapacityHandler`, `DuplicateEmailHandler`, `DuplicateHouseholdHandler`, `DuplicateIdentityHandler`, `DuplicateTelephoneHandler`\n- **429** \u2014 `DailyOwnerLimitHandler`\n\nEach now delegates to the **existing** `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail)` helper and returns `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` \u2014 which makes Spring emit `application/problem+json` with all four required members (`type=about:blank`, `title`, `status`, `detail`), status codes unchanged. `MissingOwnerFieldsHandler` additionally preserves its per-field `errors` list as a problem-detail property.\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** I reused the shared `ProblemDetails` helper rather than writing new formatting logic, so no function grew meaningfully. Every handler class contains exactly one method (WMC_other = 0) and each `handle` method stays cyclomatic-complexity 1 (no branches), so the per-function cost is just the handful of changed lines. I touched only the handler files that genuinely produce the in-scope statuses \u2014 the 404/403/500 handlers and the already-compliant `ValidationExceptionHandler` were left untouched.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest` (missing-city \u2192 400 problem+json) and the full suite both build green.",
